    "description": "Secrets management controls access to sensitive credentials and related material used by software. Cryptographic key management adds lifecycle responsibilities for keys that protect confidentiality, authenticate systems, or support digital signatures.",
    "tags": ["Secrets management", "Cryptographic keys", "Software security", "Vault", "Kubernetes"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T19:20:07Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T19:20:07Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Secrets and Cryptographic Key Management | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore credentials, key lifecycles, access controls, and Kubernetes secrets, with explicit limits on evidence about Iranian deployments."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "different-responsibilities",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Sensitive values with different purposes",
        "content": "Software depends on sensitive information such as credentials, encryption keys, and authentication certificates. HashiCorp’s Vault documentation groups these under secrets and describes centralized management across on-premises, cloud, and hybrid environments. Centralization provides a place to govern access, but the protected material can serve very different purposes. A database credential permits access to a service; a cryptographic key participates in an operation that protects or authenticates information. [s1][s2]\n\nOWASP, a nonprofit application-security community, recommends identifying the security objectives before selecting cryptographic mechanisms. Confidentiality during storage, protection during transmission, device authentication, and data-origin authentication create different requirements. Its guidance also calls for mapping the components that store or process key material. This makes key management an architectural responsibility extending beyond the choice of an encryption algorithm. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "lifecycle-and-accountability",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Managing the whole lifecycle",
        "content": "Key management covers generation, distribution, storage, rotation, recovery, and eventual destruction. OWASP treats accountability as a continuing requirement: an organization needs to understand who can access or control keys and how those keys are used. Least privilege means granting access only to roles and workloads with a verified operational need. [s2]\n\nRotation and recovery must also reflect the key's purpose. OWASP distinguishes backup considerations for encryption keys from those for digital-signature keys; it does not prescribe one universal escrow policy. The practical lesson is to document the lifecycle of each key class rather than assume that all sensitive values can be replaced or recovered in the same way. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "centralized-access",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What a secrets platform provides",
        "content": "Vault illustrates how a dedicated platform separates identity, policy, and sensitive storage. Its documentation describes authentication and authorization controls, encryption at rest, and plugins for different functions. Authentication plugins establish client access, while database plugins can manage dynamically generated credentials. Static-secret storage and certificate management are additional documented capabilities. These are product capabilities, not evidence that a particular organization has configured them effectively. [s1]\n\nFor an Iran-focused account, that distinction is essential: international documentation explains how such systems work, but does not establish local deployment, support availability, or operational outcomes. Those questions require organization-specific evidence before a provider or institution can be presented as an example.",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "kubernetes-boundary",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A Kubernetes Secret still needs protection",
        "content": "Kubernetes, a platform for managing containerized workloads, provides Secret objects for confidential information. Its security guidance states that these objects are stored unencrypted in etcd by default; etcd is the cluster's backing data store. Administrators therefore need to configure encryption at rest rather than infer protection from the object's name. [s3]\n\nAccess control is equally important. Kubernetes recommends restricting permissions to retrieve, list, or watch Secrets, and limiting access to etcd itself. Namespace separation can help isolate access to mounted secrets, while external secret stores provide another integration option. These controls address different exposure paths. Encryption of stored data does not replace authorization, and application access does not justify broad human access to every secret. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "key-lifecycle",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "Responsibilities across a key lifecycle",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[\"Define security purpose\"] -->|\"Determines requirements\"| B[\"Generate key\"]\n  B -->|\"Controlled delivery\"| C[\"Store and authorize use\"]\n  C -->|\"Lifecycle policy\"| D[\"Rotate or replace\"]\n  D -->|\"Retirement policy\"| E[\"Destroy retired material\"]\n  C -->|\"Suspected compromise\"| F[\"Investigate and recover\"]\n  F -->|\"Replacement where required\"| B\n```",
        "summary": "This conceptual lifecycle distinguishes planned replacement from a response to compromise. Specific recovery and destruction procedures depend on the key's purpose and the information it protects.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      }
    ],
